New `src/shared/registry.ts`, importing `#shared/*` only (no drizzle, no tools, no indexer):

export const REGISTRY_VERSION = 1;
export interface Registry { readonly version: number; readonly repos: readonly string[] }
export const EMPTY_REGISTRY: Registry;
export function canonicalRoot(path: string, options?: { readonly home?: string }): string;
export function readRegistry(path: string, options?: { readonly log?: Logger }): Registry;
export function writeRegistry(path: string, registry: Registry): void;
export function registerRepo(path: string, repoPath: string, options?: { readonly log?: Logger }): { readonly registry: Registry; readonly added: boolean };
export function removeRepo(path: string, repoPath: string, options?: { readonly log?: Logger }): { readonly registry: Registry; readonly removed: boolean };
export interface Selection { readonly ok: true; readonly repoRoot: string }
export interface SelectionFailure { readonly ok: false; readonly error: AppError }
export function selectRepo(input: { readonly registry: Registry; readonly requested?: string | undefined; readonly cwd: string; readonly pinnedRoot?: string | null }): Selection | SelectionFailure;

File shape, verbatim: `{"version":1,"repos":["~/Documents/a","/srv/b"]}`. `canonicalRoot` expands a leading `~/` against `options.home` (default `$HOME`), `resolve()`s, and applies `realpathSync` only when the path exists (a missing path keeps the resolved form). `readRegistry`: a missing file, an unreadable file, a non-object, a missing/!=1 `version`, or a non-array `repos` returns `EMPTY_REGISTRY` and logs exactly one `warn` with the reason; non-string entries are dropped with one `warn`; surviving entries are canonicalised and deduped with the first occurrence keeping its position. `writeRegistry` creates the parent directory recursively and writes `{version: REGISTRY_VERSION, repos}` to `<path>.tmp` then `rename`s it over `path`. `registerRepo`/`removeRepo` are read-modify-write on top of those two and swallow their own IO error into one `warn` (returning the registry they read); `added`/`removed` say whether the file had to change.

`selectRepo` order, and it is the whole contract. **An explicit request outranks a process-level pin; the pin outranks the implicit defaults.**
1. A supplied `requested` resolves against the registry: canonical path first, then basename; a basename shared by two entries is `ambiguous`, and a name matching nothing is `not_found` even when `pinnedRoot` is set (the pin is a default, not a barrier, so the documented selector never goes silently dead).
2. Otherwise a non-empty `pinnedRoot` (`--repo-root`) is canonicalised and returned without consulting the registry, which is what keeps every ad-hoc caller working.
3. Otherwise a registered root equal to `canonicalRoot(cwd)` wins, else exactly one registered repo wins, else `not_found` (none registered) or `ambiguous` (several).

Every failure is an `AppError` with a hint that lists the registered repos one per line (`sangkar repo add <path>` as the hint when the registry is empty). No new `AppErrorCode`.

New `src/shared/repo-config.ts`, `#shared/*` imports only:

export const WATCH_INTERVAL_DEFAULT_MS = 2_000;
export const WATCH_INTERVAL_MIN_MS = 1_000;
export const WATCH_INTERVAL_MAX_MS = 300_000;
export interface RepoConfig {
  readonly repoRoot: string;
  readonly dataDir: string;
  readonly watch: boolean;
  readonly watchIntervalMs: number;
  readonly telemetry: boolean;
}
export const DEFAULT_REPO_SETTINGS: { readonly watch: true; readonly watchIntervalMs: 2000; readonly telemetry: true };
export function repoConfigPath(dataDir: string): string; // join(dataDir, "config.json")
export function readRepoConfig(repoRoot: string, options?: { readonly dataDir?: string; readonly log?: Logger }): RepoConfig;
export function writeRepoConfig(config: RepoConfig): void;
export function recordingEnabled(input: { readonly consent: boolean; readonly repo: RepoConfig; readonly registered: boolean }): boolean;

File shape, verbatim: `{"watch":true,"watch_interval":2000,"telemetry":true}` (snake_case on disk, camelCase in TypeScript). `readRepoConfig` resolves `dataDir` as `options.dataDir ?? join(repoRoot, ".sangkar")` and reads `repoConfigPath(dataDir)`; a missing file, an unparseable file or a non-object yields `DEFAULT_REPO_SETTINGS` plus exactly one `warn` naming the reason and the path. Field rules: `watch`/`telemetry` must be JSON booleans, anything else takes the default (one `warn`); `watch_interval` must be an integer — a non-integer, a negative number, `0` or a wrong type falls back to `WATCH_INTERVAL_DEFAULT_MS`, while an integer in `1..999` clamps up to `WATCH_INTERVAL_MIN_MS` and one above `WATCH_INTERVAL_MAX_MS` clamps down to it. `writeRepoConfig` creates the data dir and writes the snake_case shape. `recordingEnabled` is `consent && registered && repo.telemetry` (env `off` outranks config, config `false` outranks the default) — no environment read in this file. Do not edit `src/shared/config.ts`, which keeps the machine-level consent.

In `src/shared/cursor.ts`:

export interface CursorBindings { readonly key: string; readonly repoId?: string }
export function createCursors(bindings: CursorBindings): Cursor;

`CursorPayload` and `CursorExpectation` keep exactly their four and three fields — handlers build those literals (eight `ctx.cursor.sign({...})` call sites in six handlers) and must not be edited. The signed wire payload becomes `CursorPayload & { readonly repo: string }`, where `repo` is `bindings.repoId ?? ""`. `sign()` therefore serialises five fields; `verify()` decodes the token, and when its `repo` differs from the facade's own binding it returns `{ ok: false, code: "cursor_mismatch", hint: HINT_MISMATCH }` (the existing constant — no new refusal code, no new hint text). A tampered `repo` field must keep failing the MAC as `cursor_invalid` before any comparison. `CURSOR_KEY_MIN_BYTES`, `CURSOR_TAG_BYTES`, `MAX_TOKEN_CHARS` and every hint string stay unchanged, and `src/shared/store/read/context.ts` is not edited.

New `src/shared/store/pool.ts`, generic and database-free so it is testable with fakes (`src/shared/store/*` may import `#shared/*`; this file needs neither drizzle nor a `Conn`):

export const POOL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS = 60_000;
export interface PoolEntry<T> { readonly root: string; readonly value: T; readonly openedAt: number; readonly lastUsedAt: number }
export interface StorePool<T> {
  acquire(root: string): T;                        // opens via `open` on first use, bumps lastUsedAt otherwise
  replace(root: string, value: T): void;           // stores a refreshed value; never calls `open` or `close`
  releaseIdle(now?: number): readonly string[];    // closes and forgets entries idle >= idleTimeoutMs
  closeAll(): void;
  entries(): readonly PoolEntry<T>[];
  readonly size: number;
}
export function createStorePool<T>(options: { readonly open: (root: string) => T; readonly close: (value: T) => void; readonly idleTimeoutMs?: number; readonly now?: () => number }): StorePool<T>;

`acquire` resolves the root with `resolve()` so `a`, `a/` and `./a` name one entry. No timer, no interval, no `setTimeout` inside the module: the caller drives `releaseIdle()` (the composition root on each call), which is what keeps the pool from holding a process open. `replace(root, value)` stores `value` for `root` and does nothing else: it never calls `open` and never calls `close`, so a store refreshed by `refreshStore()` replaces the old entry after the caller has closed the old one. `closeAll` is idempotent and calls `close` once per entry. A throwing `open` must not leave a half-entry behind: the next `acquire` retries the open.

